==English==
{{was wotd|2006|June|9}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|sollicit||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*solh₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|soliciten}}, {{m|enm|solliciten}}, from {{der|en|fro|soliciter}}, {{m|fro|solliciter}}, from the present active participle of {{der|en|la|sollicitō|t=stir, disturb; look after}}, from {{m|la|sollicitus|t=agitated, anxious, punctilious|lit=thoroughly moved}}, from {{m|la|sollus|t=whole, entire}} + perfect passive participle of {{m|la|cieō|t=shake, excite, cite, to put in motion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sə-lĭs'ĭt}}
* {{IPA|en|/səˈlɪsɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-solicit.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-solicit.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪsɪt|s=3}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To persistently [[endeavor]] to obtain an object, or [[bring about]] an event.
#: {{ux|en|to '''solicit''' alms, or a favour}}
#: {{syn|en|supplicate|thig}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Works|title=Eloisa to Abelard|passage=I view my crime, but kindle at the view, / Repent old pleasures, and '''solicit''' new.}}
#*: Did I '''solicit''' thee From darkness to promote me?
# To [[woo]]; to [[court]].
#: {{syn|en|address|romance}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=November|author=David Morgan|title="Piggyback"&mdash;U.S. success story|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=683|text=American railroads are not permitted to operate  long-haul road routes, but the I.C.C. decision of 1954 did permit them to '''solicit''' trailer business in, say, New York for Chicago provided the trailer was piggybacked in between.}}
# To persuade or [[incite]] one to [[commit]] some act, especially [[illegal]] or [[sexual]] behavior.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|8|passage=That fruit {{...}} '''sollicited''' her longing eye.}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding}}, Book II, Chapter 1
#*: Sounds and some tangible qualities fail not to '''solicit''' their proper senses, and force an entrance to the mind.
#: ''If you want to lose your virginity, you should try to '''solicit''' some fine looking women.''
# To offer to perform sexual activity, especially when for a [[payment]].
#: ''My girlfriend tried to '''solicit''' me for sex, but I was tired.''
# To make a [[petition]].
#: {{syn|en|appeal|request}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[disturb]] or trouble; to [[harass]].
# To urge the claims of; to [[plead]]; to act as [[solicitor]] for or with reference to.
#: {{syn|en|plead}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1628|author={{w|John Ford (dramatist)|John Ford}}|title=w:The Lover's Melancholy
|passage=Should / My brother henceforth study to forget / The vow that he hath made thee, I would ever / '''Solicit''' thy deserts.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} To [[disturb]]; to [[disquiet]].
#: {{syn|en|worry}}
#* '''1611-1615''', {{w|George Chapman}}, ''{{w|Iliad}}'', Book XVI
#*: Hath any ill '''solicited''' thine ears?
#* {{RQ:Dryden Spanish Fryar|3|2
|passage=But anxious fears '''solicit''' my weak breast.}}

